# Apple-derived binaries in the bootlab assets tree.
#
# WHY THESE ARE IGNORED, AND WHY THAT IS DELIBERATE
#
# Every file matched below is Apple's compiled product, extracted from a macOS
# dyld shared cache (or a byte-for-byte copy of one off the host disk). The
# project has a standing commitment that no Apple-derived binary is ever
# published to a remote.
#
# They are untracked, deliberately. An earlier version of this file justified
# that with the premise that the binaries "were never pushed to any remote, so
# a rewrite protected nobody". THAT PREMISE WAS WRONG, and it is recorded here
# because the correction is the reason the posture below is what it is.
#
# Measured 2026-09-28 over `git rev-list --objects origin/darwin..HEAD`:
#   - 45 Apple product binaries totalling 11,626,528 bytes were in the push
#     queue -- Apple efiboot, 40 dylibs (39 under usr/lib/system/ plus
#     libSystem.B.dylib) and the extracted dyld, all from the macOS 26.6
#     shared cache, plus byte-identical copies of the host's /bin/cat,
#     /bin/echo and /bin/ls. That is 1 + 40 + 1 + 3 = 45.
#
#     A WORD ON TWO DIFFERENT NUMBERS, because they are constantly conflated:
#     "45" counts BLOBS IN GIT (what a push would transfer). "46 dylibs",
#     further down, counts FILES ON DISK. The disk holds more than git ever
#     did, because the working copies (.orig, dyld.PRE-*, and the project's
#     own rebuilds that overwrote some extracts) were never staged. When
#     checking either number, say which one you mean.
#   - Untracking them from the tip tree did NOT unpublish them. Every one was
#     still reachable from the tip through the commit that added them, so
#     `git push` would have transferred all of it. "Untracked" and "never
#     pushed" are different properties, and only the first had been achieved.
#   - Separately, 5 copies of a project-built dyld (8,665,504 bytes) sat at
#     Libraries/dyld/dyld/dyld -- not Apple, but a build artifact, and equally
#     reachable. They have since been removed from the tree and ignored.
#
# The range has now been squashed onto origin/darwin, so nothing under
# tools/bootlab/assets/ is reachable from the published history at all. The
# rules below are what keep it that way: if a file is untracked here it
# cannot be pushed, and they stop the next `git add -A` from re-tracking it.
#
# THE FILES STAY ON DISK ON PURPOSE. assets/usr/lib/dyld is the only extracted
# dyld, and libsystem_kernel.dylib does not build, so there is no source-built
# substitute. Deleting it ends the QEMU interop test. This is an interop lab
# keeping binaries locally and unpushed -- a materially different posture from a
# repository that ships them.
#
# This is fully reversible: `git add -f` any of these paths restores tracking.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT IS OURS AND WHAT IS APPLE'S -- READ THIS BEFORE EDITING
#
# The previous version of this file listed
#     bin/{ls,echo,cat,sh}   ours (the gold binaries being replaced)
# That was FALSE, and it is the root cause of the exposure: it carved out the
# one directory that needed policing while claiming to police it. The truth:
#
#   bin/cat, bin/echo, bin/ls   APPLE PRODUCT BINARIES, on disk (they were
#       never tracked by git). Byte-for-byte identical to this host's
#       /bin/cat, /bin/echo and /bin/ls -- same size, same sha256, and each
#       SLICE's LC_UUID matches the host's slice for that architecture:
#         x86_64  8D210E93-E55B-38AD-BCCC-02E19CF504B3
#         arm64e  10F65E66-48D8-348B-BCF1-2A29606094C1
#       Note the two slices have DIFFERENT UUIDs from each other; that is
#       normal for a universal binary and is not a discrepancy. Checked with
#       `lipo -thin <arch> ... | dwarfdump --uuid` on both sides.
#
#   bin/sh                     OURS. 4,280 bytes, single-architecture x86_64,
#       LC_UNIXTHREAD with no LC_LOAD_DYLIB at all -- a freestanding static
#       image this project built. Nothing like the host's /bin/sh (101,040
#       bytes, universal x86_64+arm64e). It is NOT tracked: it was
#       untracked with the rest of bin/ on 2026-09-28. Verified:
#       `git ls-files --error-unmatch tools/bootlab/assets/bin/sh` exits 1.
#
#   usr/lib/**                 46 dylibs under usr/lib/system/ (counted
#       2026-09-28: `ls tools/bootlab/assets/usr/lib/system/*.dylib | wc -l`)
#       plus libSystem.B.dylib, plus the extracted dyld and its .orig /
#       dyld.PRE-* experiment snapshots. All Apple. NOTE: an earlier version
#       of this file said "48 dylibs"; the measured count is 46.
#   System/Library/**          2 framework binaries -- CoreFoundation.framework
#       and PrivateFrameworks/XPCSupport.framework -- extracted from the same
#       shared cache. Apple.
#
# Anything else that lands in this tree is either ours or must be added here
# before it is staged.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SCOPE: CATEGORY RULES, NOT A LIST OF FILENAMES
#
# Before this rewrite only three exact-name patterns existed
# (/usr/lib/dyld, /usr/lib/*.dylib, /usr/lib/system/*.dylib). Apple binaries
# sat outside them ON DISK -- the three bin/ tools, 18 dyld working copies,
# libSystem.B.dylib.orig, libobjc.A.dylib and the two System/ frameworks.
# An earlier version of this paragraph called that "twelve" (3+6+1+2); that
# figure matched no real state and is withdrawn.
#
# IMPORTANT: those files were never IN GIT either. `git log --all
# --diff-filter=A -- 'tools/bootlab/assets/usr/lib/dyld*'` returns exactly
# one commit and one path, usr/lib/dyld. The .orig and .PRE-* variants were
# never tracked, so the exposure was always "on disk and unignored", not
# "already committed". The rules below are written per category so that a
# newly extracted dylib, a differently named snapshot, or the next host tool
# dropped into bin/ is covered by default rather than by remembering a line.
#
# NONE of these remain tracked. Everything below is now excluded from the
# published tree, and every line the previous version of this block asserted
# about them was wrong or unverified. Corrected against measurement:
#
#   boot.efi          APPLE PROPRIETARY. 723,512 bytes. PE/COFF EFI x86-64.
#                     Carries BUILD-INFO ProjectName "efiboot", ProductName
#                     "boot.efi", Apple Inc., SdkBuild 25G74, target x86_64.
#                     It is a 3-byte-patched copy of this host's
#                     /System/Library/CoreServices/boot.efi: identical length,
#                     differing at exactly 3 offsets (0-based 59899-59901),
#                     where the function prologue `push rbp; mov rbp,rsp`
#                     (55 48 89 e5) had its FIRST THREE BYTES (55 48 89)
#                     replaced with `xor eax,eax; ret` (31 c0 c3). The host's
#                     4th prologue byte (e5) is untouched, which is why the
#                     file differs in exactly 3 byte positions.
#                     NOT EDK2, NOT OVMF, NOT open source.
#                     It is load-bearing: the only boot path is host OVMF ->
#                     UEFI Shell -> the typed command
#                     fs0:\System\Library\CoreServices\boot.efi -v serial=1
#                     so it stays on disk and is simply never committed.
#
#   BOOTX64.EFI       THIRD-PARTY, not Apple and not EDK2. Clover revision
#                     5161 (commit 015206b64), with an embedded OpenCore
#                     payload. 1,565,376 bytes. Its blob is ALREADY PUBLIC on
#                     origin at clover_extracted/CloverV2/EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.efi,
#                     so keeping a copy here publishes nothing new.
#
#   vars.fd           UNVERIFIED. 540,672 bytes. An OVMF-style variable store,
#                     but this host has no stock x86_64 OVMF var file to
#                     compare against. /usr/local/share/qemu ships
#                     edk2-{arm,i386,loongarch64,riscv}-vars.fd (and an
#                     edk2-aarch64-CODE.fd, but no aarch64 vars), and no
#                     edk2-x86_64-vars.fd at all, so the earlier claim that it
#                     is "QEMU-generated" could not be substantiated and is
#                     withdrawn. It is regenerated per run by copying a fresh
#                     var store, which is why a stale one faults at handoff.
#
#   kernel.development   OURS, but a build artifact, not source. 19,023,960
#                     bytes, built from the xnu tree in Kernel/xnu (already
#                     public under the Apple Public Source License 2.0).
#                     Stamped "Darwin Kernel Version 24.3.0: Mon Aug 31
#                     05:02:35 PDT 2026; max:build/DEVELOPMENT_X86_64".
#                     Regenerable by tools/bootlab/kernel_build.py.
#
#   template_head.bin / template_tail.bin
#                     OURS -- a golden FAT32 image head and tail, captured so
#                     mkimage.py can produce a byte-reproducible disk.
#                     template_head.bin is a DOS/MBR boot sector with a GPT
#                     protective partition (ID=0xee).
#
#   com.apple.Boot.plist, etc/rc, hello.txt
#                     OURS -- plain text config/fixtures. Retained on disk,
#                     not committed, so that no asset subtree is ever wholly
#                     absent and the lab's inputs stay self-describing.
#
#   bin/sh            OURS. 4,280 bytes, single-architecture x86_64,
#                     LC_UNIXTHREAD, no LC_LOAD_DYLIB -- a freestanding
#                     static image this project built. NOT the host's /bin/sh
#                     (101,040 bytes, universal x86_64+arm64e). It was
#                     tracked until 2026-09-28 and is now untracked with the
#                     rest of bin/, for consistency rather than necessity.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PATTERNS BELOW ARE RELATIVE TO THIS FILE's directory (tools/bootlab/assets/),
# which is git's rule for a .gitignore pattern with a leading slash. Writing
# repo-root-relative patterns here resolves them to
#   tools/bootlab/assets/tools/bootlab/assets/... which matches nothing.
# An earlier version of this file made exactly that mistake, and `git rm
# --cached` alone did not reveal it: the index stopped listing the files while
# the ignore rules were inert, so the next `git add` would have restored them.
# Verify any edit here with `git check-ignore -v <path>` from the repo root.

# (1) bin/ -- the guest userland the boot lab executes. Everything in here is
# an Apple product binary except our own sh.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE EDITING: THE `!/bin/sh` LINE BELOW IS CURRENTLY INERT,
# AND IT IS NOT THE PROTECTION YOU MIGHT ASSUME.
#
# The root .gitignore rule `/tools/bootlab/assets/*` matches the bin
# DIRECTORY itself, and git never descends into an ignored directory. So the
# four category rules in this file -- this one, the two under usr/lib/, and
# /System/ -- are not what is keeping these files out of the index today.
# The root rule is. Measured:
#   $ git check-ignore -v tools/bootlab/assets/bin
#   .gitignore:42:/tools/bootlab/assets/*   tools/bootlab/assets/bin
#   $ git check-ignore -v tools/bootlab/assets/bin/sh
#   .gitignore:42:/tools/bootlab/assets/*   tools/bootlab/assets/bin/sh
# Note what those report: the ROOT rule, not anything in this file. And the
# no-`-v` form exits 0 (ignored) for bin/sh too, not 1 -- an earlier version
# of this comment claimed otherwise and was wrong.
#
# These category rules are kept as DOCUMENTATION OF INTENT and as defence in
# depth IF the root rule is ever narrowed or removed. Do not delete the root
# rule believing this file protects the tree. Do not delete these rules
# believing they are load-bearing today, either.
/bin/*
!/bin/sh

# (2) usr/lib/ -- the dyld shared-cache extraction staging tree: 46 dylibs and
# the extracted dyld plus its working copies. The trailing globs deliberately
# cover the working copies (.orig, .PRE-*) as well as the canonical names, so an
# experiment snapshot is never accidentally committable.
/usr/lib/dyld*
/usr/lib/*.dylib*
/usr/lib/system/*.dylib*

# (3) System/ -- framework and private-framework binaries out of the same
# shared cache. Nothing of ours is staged here.
/System/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT IN THIS DIRECTORY, BUT IT WILL COME UP: `git add -A` AND Libraries/Libsystem
#
# Ignored is not the same as absent. Measured 2026-09-28,
# `git add -A --dry-run` proposes ~3,700 untracked paths, of which 2,950 are
# .o objects, 454 generated .s, and 61 are linked products worth naming:
# 17 .dylib and 44 .a under Libraries/Libsystem/, about 46.5 MB.
#
# Those 61 are THIS PROJECT'S OWN BUILD OUTPUT. They are not Apple binaries:
#   - zero "Apple Inc." stamps in any of them;
#   - their LC_ID_DYLIB is the project install path it targets
#     (/usr/lib/system/libxpc.dylib etc.), not evidence of provenance;
#   - none is byte-identical to any Apple blob that was ever in git.
#
# Two of them LOOK alarming and are not, so record it here rather than
# re-deriving it under pressure:
#   Libraries/Libsystem/libxpc/libxpc.dylib            132,672 bytes
#   Libraries/Libsystem/libsystem_m/libsystem_m.dylib   39,512 bytes
# These are byte-identical to their staged copies under
# tools/bootlab/assets/usr/lib/system/ because staging copied FROM the build
# tree. The Apple originals they replaced were much larger -- 559,470 and
# 535,784 bytes respectively -- so the size difference is itself the proof
# that the staged copies were overwritten by our own builds and are no longer
# Apple's. (libsystem_blocks and libsystem_pthread are in the same state.)
#
# And the `com.apple.*` strings inside five of these -- com.apple.quarantine,
# com.apple.ResourceFork, com.apple.metadata and friends -- are NOT evidence
# of Apple provenance. They are source literals: copyfile.c:88 defines
#   #define XATTR_ROOT_INSTALLED_NAME "com.apple.root.installed"
# and the neighbouring xattr names are the on-disk formats the library must
# read and write. A grep for "com.apple" will always hit this code.
#
# The root .gitignore deliberately has no blanket *.o / *.a rule, because
# 1,363 .o files ARE tracked fixtures under Libraries/dyld/unit-tests/. If you
# decide these should not be committed, add a scoped rule -- e.g. ignore
# /Libraries/Libsystem/**/*.a and the built dylibs -- rather than a global one.
# Until then, `git add -A` remains an informed choice, not a safe default.
